# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the Qin unite China 221 BC, the Han 202 BC to AD 220,
# Zhang Qian sent west 138 BC, paper improved AD 105; the ban liang made one currency.
lesson history-chinas-first-empire v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=4 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "China's first empire"
  goal "Say how the first emperor made one China out of many kingdoms, and count the years of the Qin and the Han across year one."

  look {
    scene 36x20 {
      column all at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=start {
        timeline line from=-300 to=200 step=100 years=[-221, -138, 105] labels=["Qin unites China", "Zhang Qian sets out", "Paper"]
        objectcard coin kind=7 name="Ban liang coin" use="Buying and selling" year=-221 about=1 place="China" ask=0
      }
    }
    say """
      For hundreds of years China was many kingdoms. In 221 BC the king of Qin conquered the last of
      them and called himself the first emperor. He made one kind of coin, round with a square hole,
      one way of writing and one set of weights for all of China, and joined old walls in the north.
      The Han who came after ruled for about four hundred years; they sent Zhang Qian west in 138 BC,
      and under them paper was improved in AD 105.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.qin-same k=0
    }
    show history.qin-same k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.china-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.china-between k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.which-century k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.qin-same k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.china-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.china-between k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.bc-ad-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.china-between k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-one-coin k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.han-times k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "In 221 BC the Qin made one China: one coin, one writing, one set of weights."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is how one ruler joined many kingdoms into an empire and made it work as one: the same
    coins, the same writing and the same measures everywhere, so that people could trade and the
    emperor could collect taxes and send orders. The way in reads what the Qin made the same; the
    core orders early China's events, counts years across year one and names centuries. The first
    emperor ruled harshly and many people were made to labour on his walls and roads; the lesson
    tells what he changed, and you can talk about the cost. The wrong idea to watch for is counting
    a year nought between 221 BC and AD 105. If the child is stuck, count to year one first. Off
    the page, look at the coins in your purse: what makes them the same everywhere in your country?
    Russia's history programme for grade five includes «Создание объединенной империи. Цинь
    Шихуанди. Возведение Великой Китайской стены. Правление династии Хань». England's key stage 2
    names "The Shang Dynasty of Ancient China" among the earliest civilisations.
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what coin all of China used, the events come nearly in order, the years counted stay before year one, and the centuries are of later events."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the questions ask why the coin had a hole and who the walls were meant to keep out, the events are fully muddled, the years counted cross year one, and the three-star question gives the dates rather than the lengths."
  }
}
